# The First Executive Functions Are Not Really “Executive” Yet

One of the easiest mistakes in developmental writing is to describe infant capacities using adult concepts without changing their meaning.

A six-month-old who sustains attention is not simply a tiny adult with immature concentration.

An infant who suppresses a reach is not displaying adult inhibitory control.

A toddler who remembers a rule is not necessarily capable of applying that rule consistently across situations.

The first three years are better understood as a period in which precursor processes become progressively coordinated.

The research dossier proposes a deliberately overlapping developmental sequence. Between birth and six months, the most relevant observations concern orienting, sustained looking, recovery from distraction, and early response modulation. Between six and twelve months, response inhibition, memory for locations or rules, and attentional shifting become increasingly visible. During the second year, context-bound inhibition, persistence, simple rule use, and symbolic guidance become more apparent. Across the third year, children show more reliable inhibition, simple planning, rule switching, private speech, goal persistence and problem solving. 

These are waves, not stages.

One ability does not finish before the next begins.

Development is overlapping, recursive, and uneven.

The child can sometimes do more than they can reliably do.

That distinction—between **capacity under particular conditions** and **stable performance across life**—will remain important throughout this chapter.

---

# Holding Something in Mind

Working memory is one of the quiet revolutions of early development.

It makes it possible for something that is not immediately present to continue influencing behavior.

Consider a simple instruction:

“Put the blue block in the box.”

The words disappear almost as soon as they are spoken.

The child must retain enough of their meaning to guide the next action. They must identify the relevant object, maintain the goal, suppress competing possibilities, and move through the environment without losing track of what they were supposed to do.

Working memory therefore creates a bridge between representation and action.

The child does not have to respond only to what is visible.

The mind can carry information forward.

That ability becomes more powerful as children acquire language, symbols and increasingly organized representations. It also becomes more demanding when tasks contain several competing instructions or when the child must update a previous rule.

The research supports the emergence of these processes during toddlerhood, but it also warns against treating them as literal storage containers with a fixed capacity. Toddler tasks place simultaneous demands on language, attention, inhibition and comprehension, making simple measurements difficult to interpret. 

This is why a child who forgets what they were told may not have “forgotten the lesson.”

The relevant representation may simply have become unavailable under the conditions of the moment.

