# Learning to Stop

Inhibitory control is the executive function adults tend to notice first because it is visible.

Do not grab.

Do not hit.

Wait.

Stop.

Stay here.

But inhibition is not the absence of impulse.

It is the ability to place one response under the control of another goal.

The toddler who wants the biscuit but waits is exercising inhibition.

The toddler who does not want it has not demonstrated the same thing.

That distinction becomes especially important in the second and third years, when children increasingly encounter situations in which immediate desire and social expectation diverge.

Early inhibitory control can be observed before age three, but it remains fragile and strongly dependent on the task and context. Children may inhibit a response under one set of circumstances and fail under another. Fatigue, frustration, novelty, emotional arousal, language comprehension, motivation and motor demands can all change performance. 

The developmental trend is nevertheless clear.

Control becomes increasingly reliable.

The child begins to pause.

And that pause creates possibilities.

---

# The Importance of Flexibility

Inhibition alone is not enough.

A child who can follow one rule but cannot change it remains rigid.

Cognitive flexibility introduces another possibility: the child can discover that the rule that worked before may no longer work now.

This is a demanding achievement.

The child must retain the previous rule, detect that circumstances have changed, suppress the old response, identify the new requirement, and shift behavior accordingly.

That is why flexibility is closely connected to working memory and inhibition.

One holds the old and new possibilities.

One suppresses what has become inappropriate.

One permits the system to change direction.

Developmental studies show increasing flexibility during the toddler and preschool years, although exact ages vary considerably with the task. What appears as “stuckness” can often represent a normal mismatch between the child's current representation and the newly introduced rule rather than a unitary deficit. 

The child is not necessarily refusing to change.

Sometimes the child simply has not yet reorganized the mental representation rapidly enough to do so.

---

# From the Outside In

Executive function is often imagined as an internal property.

Early in life, much of the organizational work is external.

Adults remind.

Adults structure.

Adults simplify.

Adults redirect attention.

Adults help children recover from frustration.

Adults provide the sequence of what happens next.

This does not mean that children passively receive regulation. The broader developmental model established in earlier chapters is reciprocal: children influence caregivers, caregivers influence children, and both operate inside material, cultural and institutional conditions. Executive development follows the same pattern. 

A useful description is therefore not that parents “install” executive function.

It is that **adults temporarily carry part of the organizational burden that children cannot yet reliably carry themselves**.

A simple reminder can function as an external memory.

A predictable routine can function as an external sequence.

A clear boundary can reduce the number of choices that must be generated in the moment.

A calm adult can help keep emotional arousal within a range in which thinking remains possible.

Over time, the child takes increasing ownership of processes that were previously shared.

The direction is toward greater self-organization, not toward independence from relationships.

