# What Children Are Learning to Do

Across the first three years, the developmental achievement is not “self-control” in the adult sense.

It is increasing coordination.

Attention can stay on a relevant object for longer.

A goal can survive interruption.

A remembered rule can influence behavior.

An immediate impulse can sometimes be restrained.

A strategy can sometimes be changed.

A child can begin talking themselves through a task.

An action can be planned rather than merely initiated.

These capacities remain uneven, but their combination changes what the child can do.

The developmental system described in the research is recursive:

**attention selects information → working memory maintains a representation → the representation guides action → inhibition suppresses competing responses → the outcome is evaluated → the child updates the next action.**

No single component guarantees success.

Motivation can interrupt the sequence.

Fatigue can weaken it.

Emotion can overwhelm it.

Language can support it.

A familiar environment can make it easier.

A supportive adult can temporarily carry part of it.

The executive system is therefore not a machine operating above the rest of development.

It is a coordination of the rest.

---

# The Problem of Measurement

This is where developmental science must resist its own seductions.

An adult sees a toddler wait.

A researcher records the behavior.

A score is produced.

A developmental label follows.

But what exactly was measured?

The child may have been demonstrating inhibitory control.

Or the child may have been responding to an especially familiar reward.

Or relying on language comprehension.

Or responding to a caregiver's presence.

Or benefiting from a well-understood game.

Or simply being unusually rested that day.

Executive-function measures in infancy and toddlerhood are vulnerable to precisely these problems. Reliability is weaker than in older children, laboratory tasks often have limited ecological validity, and the same task can draw on language, motor skills, motivation, working memory and familiarity in addition to the target construct. 

This is why multiple measures and multiple contexts are preferable.

And it is why a child's score should never become a verdict.

---

# Culture Changes the Meaning of Control

Executive function is also measured through culturally organized behavior.

What counts as good control differs across settings.

A child may be expected to wait quietly in one environment and to participate actively in another.

One community may value verbal self-assertion.

Another may place greater emphasis on deference, coordination or relational harmony.

Expectations surrounding eye contact, verbal assent, autonomy and compliance also vary culturally. The research on behavioral development therefore cautions against treating one social style as the universal expression of executive competence. 

The problem is not merely ethical.

It is methodological.

When researchers compare children across cultures, they must distinguish the executive capacity being studied from the cultural script in which that capacity is being expressed.

The question is not simply:

**Can the child control themselves?**

It is also:

**Control themselves for what purpose, under whose rules, and according to which definition of successful behavior?**

---

# The Developing Executive Is Still a Child

By the third birthday, a great deal has changed.

The child can remember.

They can wait, sometimes.

They can stop, sometimes.

They can switch.

They can solve simple problems.

They can use language to guide themselves.

They can increasingly maintain goals that extend beyond the immediate stimulus.

But this is not adult self-government.

Control is still fragile.

It remains sensitive to state, context and support.

A child who can perform beautifully in a familiar game may collapse when frustrated.

A child who remembers a rule in the kitchen may forget it on the playground.

A child who says “I won't do it” may be demonstrating emerging agency, limited flexibility, genuine preference, emotional overload—or several of these at once.

The developmental achievement lies precisely in the fact that all of these systems are beginning to become coordinated.

---

# Science, Interpretation, and Art

Science can describe executive function as a set of developing processes.

It can measure inhibition, working memory and flexibility.

It can examine developmental trajectories.

It can test interventions.

It can estimate associations with language, sleep, stress, relationships and material conditions.

It can also demonstrate how frequently popular interpretations go too far.

The marshmallow test shows that a simple behavior can be mistaken for a general trait.

Neuroscience shows that a neural correlate is not automatically a causal explanation.

Intervention research shows that improvement on a trained task is not the same thing as far transfer.

Developmental psychology repeatedly returns to the same lesson:

**measurement is not destiny.**

Interpretation takes us further.

The developing executive is the point at which human action increasingly acquires an interval.

A stimulus appears.

A desire arises.

A memory remains active.

A rule is recalled.

And somewhere between wanting and doing, another possibility begins to exist.

The child can hesitate.

They can consider.

They can try another way.

Art begins where science can no longer tell us exactly what that interval means.

Perhaps it is the beginning of choice.

Perhaps it is the beginning of selfhood.

Perhaps it is simply the moment when the mind becomes capable of representing a different action before carrying one out.

Whatever language we choose, the change is profound.

The child is becoming able to act not only because something is happening now, but because something has been remembered, represented, imagined or intended.

That is the beginning of a new kind of freedom.

---

# What the First Three Years Establish

Executive function does not arrive as a finished executive.

It develops as a gradual coordination of systems that began much earlier.

The child first learns within regulation.

Then through attention.

Then through action and memory.

Then through language and shared symbols.

Then through rules, expectations and social participation.

Executive function brings these capacities increasingly into coordination.

By age three, the foundations are visible. Children can increasingly maintain representations, inhibit some competing responses, shift between rules, use language and symbols to guide behavior, and solve simple problems. But the control remains uneven, state-dependent and highly context-sensitive. Early differences are possibilities, not verdicts. 

The most important developmental shift is therefore not that the child has learned to obey.

It is that the child is beginning to acquire the capacity to organize their own action around something that is not simply the immediate present.

A remembered goal can compete with an impulse.

A word can alter an action.

A symbol can stand in for reality.

A plan can reach forward in time.

The mind is no longer merely responding to the world.

It is beginning to carry another world inside it.

And once a child can act on what is represented rather than only on what is immediately present, a new developmental territory opens.

**The territory of imagination.**
